A||||4||Blaster||Driving me crazy....||||8||
Z||000000||Blaster||09-05-2000||11:57 PM||||OK.... this is a problem that seems to be eluding me.  It is something with my coil.  I tried 2 accell coils and with both of them my car would start backfiring and die after a couple of hours.  I went back to old coil and car ran ok.  Then a few days ago I noticed that I was getting a arc off of my coil to my exhaust shield.(v6)  So I went to auto zone and bought a STOCK coil.  After about a day, the damn thing did the same as the accells and left me on the side of the road at 9 at night.  Luckily I kept my old coil with me and was able to get it on there (working in the dark) with a couple of burns and cuss words.  With my stock coil on I am getting some back fires and car tends to studder when I get on it.  If ANYONE can help me, it would be appreciated. Also, I have accell 8mm superstock wires and ACdelco rapid fire plugs on it.||||reg||8
Z||000001||stimpy||09-06-2000||12:10 AM||||I'm stumped. I have the same set up (Accel supercoil, superstock wires) and have had zero problems. My initial thought is that you have bad wires, but if the superstocks are relatively new, I can't see how that would do it. Possibly a bad boot? Take a close look at the coil wire, the extra juice from the supercoil may cause an arc when the stock coil might not.  But it does sound like you have issues no matter what coil you use, so I would look elsewhere besides the coil. Hope this helps!<P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||WisconsinGT||09-06-2000||12:34 AM||||I had a similar problem when I put my Accel ciol on my 2.8. It turned out to be my wires. I know that is not your case, but I do know when someone puts something new on there engine, you will more then likely find out you have another problem somewhere in the link. So knowing that, I would check your cap/rotor. Then maybe your control modual. Good luck! ||||reg||1
Z||000003||capt_ron||09-06-2000||10:29 AM||||Sounds like cross firing under the distributor cap. You may have carbon tracks that have enough resistance that the stock coil won't fire across them. However the high powered coil can jump these tracks with ease and fire the wrong cylinders at the wrong time. That's why you get the backfiring. Remove the cap and look in it under a bright light, you will probably see the tracks from the center post out. <P>------------------<BR>CAPT RON...<BR>Jack of Trades,<BR>Master of None.<BR>87 SE, 2.8L, Getrag.||||reg||1
Z||000004||whamel||09-06-2000||10:39 AM||||Just because they're new doesn't mean that your plug wires can't be bad!  Check them out before you rule them out.||||reg||1
